      "abstract": "Abstract It is ironic, to say the least, that for a long time now the little known name of Nikolai Aleksandrovich Rozhkov (1868\u20131927) has been used to substantiate a range of claims about the world's most famous revolutionary \u2013 Lenin. Despite numerous one\u2010line references, so little has been written about Rozhkov that readers are unable to evaluate the validity of any statement written about him or his relationship to others, including Lenin. This article attempts to begin to address this issue by providing a very brief biography of Rozhkov, with particular reference to his revolutionary work in the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party (RSDLP) and his relationship with Lenin up to 1908. Notes See R. Hellie's article on CitationRozhkov in Wieczynski, ed., The Modern Encyclopedia of Russian and Soviet History, 217\u201321. CitationPipes, ed., The Unknown Lenin, 11\u201312; CitationVolkogonov, Lenin, 197, 362\u20133; CitationVolkogonov, The Rise and Fall of the Soviet Empire, 6\u20138; CitationService, Lenin, 459, 461. CitationMcNeal, Bride of the Revolution, 190. CitationService, Lenin, 458. See also CitationVolkogonov, Lenin, 416. CitationService, Lenin, 459. RTsKhIDNI, f.2, op.2, d.134, reprinted in Rodina no. 3 (1992), 49. CitationVolkogonov, The Rise and Fall of the Soviet Empire, 8. CitationPipes, ed., The Unknown Lenin, 12. CitationVolkogonov, Lenin, 103. CitationBarber, Soviet Historians in Crisis, 13. See also CitationBaron, Plekhanov, 39. N.A. Rozhkov, \u2018Avtobiografiia\u2019, in \u2018Pamiati N.A. Rozhkova\u2019, Katorga i ssylka no. 3 (Citation1927), 161. Rozhkov wrote his short autobiography in 1924 for the Granat entsiklopedicheskii slovar'. See CitationWolfe, Three Who Made a Revolution, 52\u201366. CitationRozhkov, \u2018Avtobiografiia\u2019, 161. Rozhkov, loc. cit. Rozhkov refers to this as the \u2018protsess pervomartovtsev\u2019; i.e. \u2018the events relating to the first of March\u2019. Rozhkov, loc. cit. The f